Different Argo CD users should have different permissions.
For example, Jai can add clusters, while Ali can manage applications only inside the kia-project project.
1. The RBAC policy format

The slide shows two policy formats:

For applications, logs, and exec:

p, <role/user/group>, <resource>, <action>, <project>/<object>

For other resources:

p, <role/user/group>, <resource>, <action>, <object>

Think of it as:

WHO        WHAT             DO WHAT        WHERE
role       resource         action         object

Example:

p, role:kia-admins, applications, *, kia-project/*

means:

The kia-admins role can perform any action (*) on applications inside kia-project.

2. Jai — Cluster administrator

The first policy is:

p, role:create-cluster, clusters, create, *

This gives the role:

role:create-cluster

permission to:

resource = clusters
action   = create
object   = *

So Jai can create/register clusters.

That's why:

argocd account can-i create clusters '*'

returns:

yes

But:

argocd account can-i delete clusters '*'

returns:

no
Important!

Jai has:

CREATE cluster       ✅
DELETE cluster       ❌

So this is least-privilege RBAC rather than giving Jai full admin access.

3. Ali — Project administrator

The second policy is:

p, role:kia-admins, applications, *, kia-project/*

This means:

Role:       kia-admins
Resource:   applications
Action:     *
Project:    kia-project
Object:     *

So Ali can perform all application actions within the kia-project.

For example:

Sync application       ✅
Delete application     ✅
Create application     ✅
Update application     ✅

But importantly, this permission is scoped to:

kia-project/*

It does not automatically give Ali permission over applications in every Argo CD project.

4. KIA team

The KIA team inherits the permissions associated with:

role:kia-admins

So the structure is roughly:

             Argo CD
                │
          ┌─────┴─────┐
          │   RBAC    │
          └─────┬─────┘
                │
       ┌────────┴────────┐
       │                 │
      Jai               Ali
       │                 │
       ▼                 ▼
create clusters     kia-project apps
       │                 │
       ▼                 ▼
    Cluster          Applications
5. The final command

Ali runs:

argocd account can-i sync applications kia-project/*

and gets:

yes

That means:

Ali is authorized to sync applications belonging to kia-project.

🔥 Very important interview concept

Don't confuse Kubernetes RBAC with Argo CD RBAC.

Kubernetes RBAC

Controls access to Kubernetes resources:

User
  ↓
Role / ClusterRole
  ↓
Pods
Deployments
Services
Secrets
...
Argo CD RBAC

Controls what a user can do inside Argo CD:

User
  ↓
Argo CD Role
  ↓
Applications
Projects
Clusters
Repositories
Logs
Exec
...

So you can have:

Jai → Can create clusters in Argo CD
Ali → Can manage applications in kia-project
Kia team → Can sync applications in kia-project

without making everyone an Argo CD administrator.

Easy way to remember
Argo CD RBAC = "What can this person do in Argo CD?"


Kubernetes RBAC = "What can this person do inside Kubernetes?"

And the policy:
p, role, resource, action, object

What:
RBAC = Role-Based Access Control. It controls what users/groups can do inside Argo CD.

Policy format:

p, <role/user/group>, <resource>, <action>, <object>

Example:

p, role:kia-admins, applications, *, kia-project/*
